n't mention anything like that, so I think dividing directly is fine. 420 divided by 10 is exactly 42 pages. So he needs to buy 42 pages. \n\nLet me check my calculations again to make sure. Total cards: 60 packs * 7 cards per pack. 60*7 is indeed 420. Pages per card: 10. 420 divided by 10 is 42. Yep, that seems right. So the answer is 42 pages.\n\nI think that's it. No steps seem to be missed. The key was converting the total number of cards into pages by dividing them by the capacity per page. Since there's no mention of needing more pages or less, the answer is 42.\n</think>\n\nPunger buys 60 packs of baseball cards, each with 7 cards. The total number of cards is:\n\n$$\n60 \\times 7 = 420\n$$\n\nEach page can hold 10 cards, so the number of pages required is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{420}{10} = 42\n$$\n\nThus, Punger needs to buy $\\boxed{42}$ pages.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 7411, 13, 220, 1260, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 13, 8886, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 4766, 13, 1260