ips. Each trip, they can carry 3 bags. So 5 trips with 3 bags each would be 15 bags. But we only need 30 bags. Wait, that seems like a problem. Maybe I did it wrong.\n\nWait, hold on. If each trip Elysse can carry 3, and her brother can carry the same amount, so 3 total per trip? So per trip, total of 3 + 3 = 6 bags. Then 30 divided by 6 is 5 trips. So 5 trips. That would take 5 trips. But 5 times 6 bags is 30. Oh, so 30 bags over 5 trips, that's correct. So my initial answer seems right.\n\nBut let me make sure. What's the question again? \"How many trips will it take them to carry 30 bags of groceries?\"\n\nSo yes, the answer is 5. Therefore, the final answer is 5. So I think that's it. I don't think there's any trick here. Maybe they can carry each trip the same amount, so 3 bags. The key is that both can carry 3 per trip, so together they can carry 6. Therefore, the answer is 30 divided by 6, which is 5. Yeah, that seems straightforward.\n\n**Final Answer**\nIt will take \\boxed{5} trips.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many trips Elysse and her brother will need to take to carry 30 bags of groceries, we first understand the number of bags they can carry per trip.\n\n- Elysse and her brother each can carry **3 bags of groceries** per trip.\n\nSo, together, they can carry:\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\text{bags} + 3 \\, \\text{bags} = 6 \\, \\text{bags per trip}\n$$\n\nNow, we are tasked with finding out how many such trips are needed to carry **30 bags** of groceries:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total trips} = \\frac{30 \\text{ bags}}{6 \\text{ bags per trip}} = 5 \\text{ trips}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n$$\n\\boxed{5}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Elysse can carry 3 bags of groceries into her home with each trip from the car. Her brother can carry the same amount. How many trips will it take them to carry 30 bags of groceries?", "answer": "Elysse and her brother can carry 3 x 2 = <<3*2=6>>6 bags of groceries with each trip from the car.\nTherefore, it will take both of them 30/6 = <<30/6=5>>5 trips.\n#### 5", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Elysse can carry 3 bags of groceries into her home with each trip from the car. Her brother can carry the same amount. How many trips will it take them to carry 30 bags of groceries?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["5"], "solution": "5"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 468, 60392, 325, 646, 6777, 220, 18, 17899, 315, 65408, 1119, 1059, 2114, 448, 1817, 8411, 504, 279, 1803, 13, 6252, 10641, 646, 6777, 279, 1852, 3311, 13, 2585, 1657, 22466, 686, 432, 1896, 1105, 311, 6777], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 468, 60392, 325, 323, 1059, 10641, 646, 1817, 6777, 220, 18, 17899, 315, 65408, 817, 8411, 13, 576, 3491, 374, 10161, 1246, 1657, 22466, 807, 1184, 311, 1281, 369, 220, 18, 15, 17899, 13, 6771, 752, 1744, 911, 419, 3019, 553, 3019, 382, 5338, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 22466, 1817, 1697, 1558, 304, 2790, 13]}
