ny chance I made a mistake with the days? Let me think. A year is 365 days, which is correct. Yep. So, the calculation seems right. I don't see any errors in this reasoning. Therefore, Harris will spend $146 on carrots in one year.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by computing the total number of carrots Harris feeds in one year:\n\n- Harris eats 1 carrot per day.\n- There are 365 days in a year.\n\n$$ \\text{Total number of carrots} = 365 \\text{ carrots/day} \\times 1 \\text{ carrot/day} = 365 \\text{ carrots} $$\n\nNext, we determine how many 5-carrot bags are needed. Since each bag contains 5 carrots, the number of bags is:\n\n$$ \\text{Number of bags} = \\frac{365 \\text{ carrots}}{5 \\text{ carrots/bag}} = 73 \\text{ bags} $$\n\nEach bag costs $2.00, so the total cost is:\n\n$$ \\text{Total cost} = 73 \\text{ bags} \\times 2.00 \\text{ dollars/bag} = 146 \\text{ dollars} $$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{146}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?", "answer": "His dog gets 1 carrot a day and there are 365 days in a year, so his dog eats 1*365 = <<1*365=365>>365 carrots\nThere are 5 carrots per bag and he will need 365 carrots, so that's 365/5 = 73 bags of carrots\nThe bags cost $2.00 and he will need 73 bags so that's $2*73 = $<<2*73=146.00>>146.00 worth of organic carrots\n#### 146", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Harris feeds his dog 1 large organic carrot over the course of 1 day.  There are 5 carrots in a 1 pound bag and each bag costs $2.00.  In one year, how much will Harris spend on carrots?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["146"], "solution": "146"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 3