liters divided by tank capacity. That would be 10,000 liters divided by 500 liters per tank. Let me do that division. 10,000 divided by 500.\n\nHmm, 500 times 20 is 10,000. So, 10,000 divided by 500 is 20. Therefore, he needs 20 tanks.\n\nWait, let me check again. 20 tanks at 500 liters each would be 20 * 500 = 10,000 liters. Yep, that matches the total liters needed. So, that makes sense.\n\nSo, the answer should be 20. But let me see if there's another way to approach this, just to confirm. Maybe something with proportions?\n\nAnother way: Let me express the amount of air in terms of tanks. If he needs 10,000 liters, and each tank is 500 liters, then the number of tanks is 10,000 / 500 = 20. Same result. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, since 500 liters is a little bigger than 10 liters per balloon, so to get 10,000 liters, you would need more than 500 tanks. But since 500 tanks give exactly 10,000 liters, that's correct. So, maybe 20 is the right answer.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me just make sure I didn't misread the problem. The problem says he buys 500-liter tanks, so that's the capacity. Each balloon is 10 liters. The number of balloons is 1000. So, total air is indeed 10,000 liters. Divided by 500 liters per tank, 20 tanks. Yup. So, the answer should be 20.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many 500-liter tanks John needs to buy, we start by calculating how much air is required to fill all the balloons.\n\nEach balloon holds 10 liters of air, and there are 1000 balloons:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total air needed} = 10 \\, \\text{liters/balloon} \\times 1000 \\, \\text{balloons} = 10,000 \\, \\text{liters}\n$$\n\nJohn is to fill all the balloons, and he needs tanks that hold **500 liters** each. Thus, the number of tanks he needs to buy can be calculated by dividing the total air required by the capacity of each tank:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000 \\, \\text{liters}}{500 \\, \\text{liters/tank}} = 20 \\, \\text{tanks}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 3757, 6801, 311, 3695, 69480, 323, 25866, 311, 5155, 1105, 705, 13, 6771, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 419, 700, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 5512, 11, 1077, 752, 1349, 279, 3491, 1549, 311, 1281, 2704, 358, 3535, 432, 12440, 382, 13079, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 1416]}
