2026-10-30 05:22:00 INFO areno.api.trainers.policy_only.PolicyOnlyTrainer policy_only.py:723 - rollout_completion={"kind": "rollout", "epoch": 0, "step": 42, "prompt_idx": 0, "sample_idx": 0, "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "decoded_prompt": "<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n", "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. So Jame has a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week. His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours a week. The question is asking how much more money he makes per year in his new job compared to the old job if he works 52 weeks a year.\n\nAlright, first, I need to figure out how much he makes in both jobs per week and then compare the annual totals. Let me start with the new job.\n\nFieldwork for the new job: each hour he's getting $20, so for 40 hours a week, his weekly income should be 40 times 20. Let me calculate that. 40 times 20... 40*20 is 800. So his new weekly salary is $800.\n\nWait, but he worked 40 hours a week, meaning he work 40 hours per week. So yeah, 40*20=800. Okay.\n\nNow for the old job, his previous salary was $16 per hour, working 25 hours a week. So 25 hours times $16. Let me compute that. 25*16. Hmm, 25*10 is 250, and 25*6 is 150, so total is 250+150=400. So his old weekly salary was $400.\n\nSo the difference between his new income per week and the old one is 800 - 400 = 400 dollars per week. That seems right. Now, he works 52 weeks a year, so to find his annual earnings, multiply by 52.\n\nSo 52 weeks * 400 dollars per week. Let me calculate that. 50*400 is 20,000, and 2*400 is 800, so total is 20,000 + 800 = $20,800 per year.\n\nTherefore, the difference in his annual earnings is $20,800.\n\nLet me check if I did everything right. Wait, original problem says \"how much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job\". So the answer should be the difference, which is 20,800 dollars. But let me verify once more.\n\nNew weekly salary: 40*20=800. Old weekly salary: 25*16=400. Difference per week: 800-400=400. Multiply by 52 weeks: 400*52. Let me compute that again. 400*50=20,000 and 400*2=800, so total is 20,800. Yes, same result.\n\nSo the answer is $20,800. So in boxed form, \\boxed{20800}. I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{20800}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we first determine the weekly earnings of Jame in both his new and old jobs and then calculate the annual earnings based on the given number of weeks.\n\n---\n\n### **New Job:**\n\n- New hourly rate: $20  \n- Work hours per week: 40  \n- Weekly earnings:  \n  $$\n  40 \\text{ hours} \\times 20 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 800 \\text{ dollars}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### **Old Job:**\n\n- Old hourly rate: $16  \n- Work hours per week: 25  \n- Weekly earnings:  \n  $$\n  25 \\text{ hours} \\times 16 \\text{ dollars/hour} = 400 \\text{ dollars}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### **Annual Earnings:**\n\n- Jame works 52 weeks a year  \n- Weekly earnings difference:  \n  $$\n  800 \\text{ dollars} - 400 \\text{ dollars} = 400 \\text{ dollars/week}\n  $$  \n  Annual earnings:\n  $$\n  400 \\text{ dollars/week} \\times 52 \\text{ weeks} = 20,800 \\text{ dollars}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20800}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?", "answer": "He makes 20*40=$<<20*40=800>>800 per week\nHe used to make 16*25=$<<16*25=400>>400 per week\nSo his raise was 800-400=$<<800-400=400>>400 per week\nSo he makes 400*52=$<<400*52=20800>>20,800 per year more\n#### 20800", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20800"], "solution": "20800"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 92088, 5221, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 323, 4278, 220, 19, 15, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 220, 5301, 2310, 2618, 572, 400, 16, 21, 458, 6460, 369, 220, 17, 20, 4115, 817, 2003, 13], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 92088, 702, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 323, 4278, 220, 19, 15, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 5301, 2310, 2618, 572, 400, 16, 21, 458, 6460, 369, 220, 17, 20, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 576, 3405, 374, 10161, 1246, 1753, 803, 3220, 566, 3643, 817, 1042, 304, 806, 501, 2618, 7707, 311, 279]}
