. I don't think there's any catch here. Maybe the problem is straightforward subtraction of the total sold boxes from the required number. Yeah, I think that's correct.\n</think>\n\nKaylee needs to sell a total of 33 boxes of biscuits. She has already sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits. Adding these gives:  \n$$ 12 + 5 + 4 = 21 $$  \nSince she needs a total of 33 boxes, the number of boxes she needs to sell more is:  \n$$ 33 - 21 = 12 $$  \n\nThus, Kaylee needs to sell **12 more boxes**.  \n\n$$\n\\boxed{12}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?", "answer": "The number of boxes already sold is 12 + 5 + 4 = <<12+5+4=21>>21.\nKaylee needs to sell 33 − 21 = 12 more boxes.\n#### 12", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["12"], "solution": "12"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30145, 8501, 3880, 311, 4559, 220, 18, 18, 14697, 315, 85976, 13, 2055, 3041, 11, 1340, 702, 6088, 220, 16, 17, 14697, 315, 29464, 85976, 311, 1059, 50066, 11, 220, 20, 14697, 315, 17931, 85976, 311, 1059], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 30145, 8501, 3880, 311, 4559, 220, 18, 18, 14697, 315, 85976, 304, 2790, 13, 2932, 2669, 6088, 220, 16, 17